$OctFourArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'writable-food-prep-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Writable Food Prep Labels for Real Kitchen Shifts'
    Description = 'Plan writable food prep labels around cold containers, pen or printer compatibility, changing dates, clean removal and kitchen hand-offs.'
    Category = 'Foodservice operations'
    CardDescription = 'A prep label should make the next shift certain about the container, not leave them guessing at faded handwriting.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of illustrated writable food prep labels on clear kitchen containers with legible blank date and initials fields'
    Caption = 'A useful writable area must stay readable after chilling, wiping and a shift change.'
    Published = '2026-10-04'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can any marker write on a food prep label?', 'No. Coating, ink and moisture affect drying and smearing. Test the actual pen or printer on the proposed label after the normal storage interval.'),
      @('Should the label be removable after washing?', 'That depends on the container reuse process. Trial adhesion during use and removal with the real cleaning method before specifying a construction.'),
      @('Who decides the date wording?', 'The food business owns its date-control procedure and market-specific requirements. The print supplier should reproduce approved fields, not assign shelf life.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A clear tub moves from prep bench to walk-in cooler, then into the hands of another shift. If the label says only “sauce” in damp handwriting, the next worker has to ask what was made and when. I would design the writable food prep label around that hand-off, not around a pretty empty rectangle.</p>
  <p>Decide which information is always printed and which is added each day. A simple original herb icon can help staff identify a family of contents, but the product name, date fields and initials need most of the space. The kitchen must approve its own date-control rules; a label supplier cannot decide how long prepared food remains usable.</p>
  <h2>Start With the Container and the Writing Tool</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What to test</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where is the label applied?</td><td>Real dry or chilled container</td><td>Surface and temperature affect hold</td></tr><tr><td>What writes the data?</td><td>Actual pen, marker or printer</td><td>Ink may smear on the coating</td></tr><tr><td>Who reads it next?</td><td>Container on a crowded shelf</td><td>Text must be visible at working distance</td></tr><tr><td>How is it removed?</td><td>Normal wash or disposal routine</td><td>Residue can slow reuse</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Try the longest realistic product name and the messiest plausible handwriting on a full-size proof. Small fields encourage abbreviations that different shifts may not understand. Leave enough contrast for a black pen without making the printed artwork so dark that fresh writing disappears.</p>
  <h2>Trial the Full Kitchen Cycle</h2>
  <p>Apply sample labels after the same surface preparation staff normally use. Write on them, place them in the cooler, handle them with damp hands and wipe the container as usual. Then remove the label using the actual cleaning method. A sample that looks excellent on a dry desk proves very little about a reusable kitchen tub.</p>
  <p>Paper, film and removable adhesive are options to review, not universal answers. The container may be polypropylene, glass or stainless steel. A label can hold well on one and fail on another. If the kitchen uses thermal-transfer or direct-thermal printing for variable data, confirm that printer, ribbon or printhead and label construction together.</p>
  <h2>Make Version Control a Shift Habit</h2>
  <p>Keep the date format and field order consistent across batches. If the business changes its prep procedure, retire old rolls rather than leaving two label versions beside the printer. The human cost of inconsistent fields is the pause before someone trusts the container.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send a photo of the actual filled container</li><li>List fixed text and changing fields separately</li><li>Test the intended pen or printer at final size</li><li>Check legibility after cold and damp handling</li><li>Trial removal with the real wash routine</li><li>Approve date wording through the food business</li></ul>
  <p>Use the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable adhesive guide</a> to frame cleaning questions and the <a href="/blog/thermal-transfer-printable-labels-guide/">overprint guide</a> if data is added by printer. Send container photos and approved field names through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. The best prep label makes the next shift's decision easier.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'labels-for-recycled-kraft-cartons-guide'
    Title = 'Labels on Recycled Kraft Cartons: Test the Surface'
    Description = 'Plan custom labels for recycled kraft cartons around rough fibers, dust, print contrast, adhesive trials and careful sustainability claims.'
    Category = 'Kraft packaging'
    CardDescription = 'A rough brown carton is not a smooth white proof; test hold and readability on the production stock.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated botanical product labels applied to textured recycled kraft cartons on a packing table'
    Caption = 'The fiber texture and background color change both adhesion and the look of printed artwork.'
    Published = '2026-10-04'
    Faq = @(
      @('Will a standard paper label stick to every recycled carton?', 'No. Fiber texture, coating, dust and storage conditions vary. Test the proposed adhesive on the actual production carton.'),
      @('Can the pack claim the label is recyclable?', 'Only when the specific label construction, carton and local collection route support that claim. The packaging owner must approve the wording.'),
      @('Does a kraft carton change printed color?', 'It can change the overall visual impression and the contrast around the label. Proof the artwork on the real brown carton, not only against a white monitor background.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A kraft carton can make a product look grounded and simple. It can also expose a label specification that was approved only on smooth white board. Fibers rise from the surface, dust remains after folding and the brown background changes the apparent color of a pale illustration. I would bring an actual production carton to the artwork review.</p>
  <p>Recycled content does not describe one uniform surface. Ask whether the carton is uncoated, coated, heavily textured or handled through a dusty packing process. The label needs a reasonable contact area on the assembled box, clear of fold lines and high-rub edges.</p>
  <h2>Inspect the Carton Before Choosing a Label</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Pack feature</th><th>Possible issue</th><th>Physical check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Raised fibers</td><td>Reduced contact at edges</td><td>Apply a final-size sample</td></tr><tr><td>Dust from folding</td><td>Unpredictable adhesion</td><td>Test after normal packing</td></tr><tr><td>Dark brown stock</td><td>Low contrast</td><td>View artwork at shelf distance</td></tr><tr><td>Box score or flap</td><td>Movement and lifting</td><td>Open and close the labeled box</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>A fully illustrated botanical label with a light ground can give a brand a clear focal point without covering the entire carton. Use original art and make the product name large enough to survive the brown frame around it. A transparent label may let the kraft texture show through, but fine lettering can lose contrast; test the printed sample on the actual stock.</p>
  <h2>Check Hold After Normal Handling</h2>
  <p>Apply the label at the same stage the packing team will use. Press it as they would, stack cartons, move them into their shipping case and inspect the corners later. If the box is refrigerated, humid or handled with oily hands, include that condition. Do not promise adhesion from a material name alone.</p>
  <p>If the sticker is a closure seal, separate two jobs: presenting the brand and keeping the flap shut. A decorative label can look good while a weak closure opens in transit. Test the seal across the real assembled flap. A different shape or contact area may be more useful than a heavier-looking illustration.</p>
  <h2>Keep Sustainability Copy Evidence-Based</h2>
  <p>“Recycled carton” is not proof that the whole labeled pack is recyclable or compostable. The packaging owner should confirm material composition, adhesive and the collection route before printing an environmental claim. Leave unverified badges off the proof. It is easier to add approved copy later than to defend a claim made for decoration.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send samples of the final folded carton</li><li>Mark score lines, flap and usable flat panels</li><li>Proof color and type on the brown stock</li><li>Test adhesion after normal dust and handling</li><li>Check closure separately if the label seals a flap</li><li>Approve every environmental statement with evidence</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">surface-matching guide</a> for adhesive questions and the <a href="/blog/custom-bakery-box-stickers-guide/">box sticker guide</a> for closure placement. Share the actual carton and approved artwork through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The carton is part of the proof, not just the background.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'automatic-label-applicator-first-run-guide'
    Title = 'Automatic Label Applicator Setup: First-Run Checklist'
    Description = 'Prepare a custom label roll for an automatic applicator with the right core, unwind, gap, sensor, peel behavior and on-line trial.'
    Category = 'Production line setup'
    CardDescription = 'A print-perfect roll can still stop the line if its winding or sensing does not match the applicator.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of fully printed original fruit drink labels feeding through an automatic bottle label applicator during a setup trial'
    Caption = 'The first approved roll should be observed on the actual applicator, not only measured on a bench.'
    Published = '2026-10-04'
    Faq = @(
      @('Is matching the core size enough for an applicator?', 'No. Unwind direction, outside diameter, web width, label gap, liner and sensor response also need confirmation.'),
      @('Can a hand-applied label roll run automatically?', 'Only after a machine trial. A hand-friendly roll may have a different liner, perforation or winding than the applicator needs.'),
      @('Who approves the first production roll?', 'The line owner should check a representative run on the actual machine, with the final container and an agreed reject or rework process.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>The label artwork is approved, the color looks right and the roll arrives on time. Then the applicator cannot see the gap between labels. The line stops for a reason no one considered during the design meeting. I would ask for the machine specifications before promising a roll is ready for automatic application.</p>
  <p>The first-run checklist is not a substitute for the machine manual or operator. It is a bridge between the packaging buyer, printer and line team. Record the applicator model, sensor type, core, maximum roll diameter, label orientation and the container that will actually be labeled.</p>
  <h2>Confirm the Web Before Printing Bulk</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Confirm with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Core and roll diameter</td><td>Roll must mount and clear guards</td><td>Machine manual or operator</td></tr><tr><td>Unwind and label orientation</td><td>Artwork must face the correct way</td><td>Annotated roll sketch</td></tr><tr><td>Gap and web width</td><td>Sensor and guides need consistency</td><td>Trial roll</td></tr><tr><td>Liner type and release</td><td>Label must separate at peel plate</td><td>On-line observation</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>For an original fruit drink label, send a diagram showing the bottle's front panel and the direction of travel. “Labels out” can still leave the artwork upside down at the applicator. Use a physical sample to verify the drawing. If the line uses a camera or barcode check, include the final printed mark in the trial.</p>
  <h2>Watch the Whole First Run</h2>
  <p>Load a representative short roll, start at the normal setup speed and then observe the intended operating range. Check sensor misses, web tracking, label skew, bubbles, liner breaks and the waste rewind. Apply labels to actual containers, including the first and last units of the trial. A label that peels cleanly but lands across a bottle seam is still not approved.</p>
  <p>Do not convert a handful of successful applications into a guarantee about full-run output. Record the conditions and any adjustment made: sensor setting, guide position, pressure, speed and container variation. The line team owns machine approval and safety; the label supplier owns the construction it provided.</p>
  <h2>Freeze the Approved Specification</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Collect machine model and operator-approved limits</li><li>Confirm core, outside diameter and web width</li><li>Approve unwind and artwork orientation on a sketch</li><li>Check sensor detection on the intended liner</li><li>Run the label on final containers at the line</li><li>Save trial settings and reject examples for reorder</li></ul>
  <p>Use the <a href="/blog/label-roll-unwind-direction-core-size-guide/">core and unwind guide</a> for the roll drawing and the <a href="/blog/label-liner-choice-guide/">liner guide</a> for sensing and release questions. Send the machine specification and container photo through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The first roll should teach the team something before the full order does.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'shipping-carton-identification-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Shipping Carton ID Labels: Scan Before the Pallet Moves'
    Description = 'Plan B2B shipping carton identification labels around corrugated boxes, changing SKU data, barcode readability and warehouse placement.'
    Category = 'Warehouse labeling'
    CardDescription = 'Give pickers and receivers a consistent carton face for SKU and shipment data.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of corrugated shipping cartons with original printed SKU identification labels and clear sample barcodes at a warehouse bench'
    Caption = 'Illustrative sample IDs are shown; actual codes must be supplied and scan-tested by the shipment owner.'
    Published = '2026-10-04'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can the carton label go across a tape seam?', 'Avoid assuming it can. Opening, tape edges and box movement can damage the label or code; test a stable panel on the assembled carton.'),
      @('Can the printer create carton barcodes from a product name?', 'The shipment or product owner must supply the identifier rules and approved data. The label supplier should not invent codes or logistics standards.'),
      @('Is a readable barcode on a screen enough?', 'No. Scan the final printed code on a real packed carton under the receiving workflow, including stacks and typical handling.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A warehouse carton is rarely viewed straight-on under perfect light. It is stacked, turned, taped and sometimes checked in a hurry at the dock. A useful shipping carton ID label gives the next person a predictable place to find the right SKU and shipment reference. I would stand where the receiver stands before deciding which box face gets the sticker.</p>
  <p>Separate product identity from shipment-specific data. The brand may print a fixed original icon or product family cue, while SKU, variant, lot or destination information changes. The shipping or product owner must define those identifiers and any barcode format. A label printer should never invent a code that merely looks scannable.</p>
  <h2>Choose a Face That Survives Packing</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Carton feature</th><th>Risk</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tape seam</td><td>Opening damages the code</td><td>Place away from tape edges</td></tr><tr><td>Box corner</td><td>Label wraps or creases</td><td>Use a stable flat panel</td></tr><tr><td>Stack orientation</td><td>ID disappears inside a pallet</td><td>View the real stack</td></tr><tr><td>Corrugated surface</td><td>Dust or roughness affects hold</td><td>Trial after packing</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Decide whether one face is enough or whether the warehouse needs two identical IDs on adjacent sides. More labels increase cost and application work, so make that choice from the actual storage and receiving process. Keep human-readable text near a code so a worker has a fallback when a scanner is unavailable.</p>
  <h2>Proof the Variable Data, Not Only the Frame</h2>
  <p>Test the longest expected SKU, a realistic variant name and several sample data records. Leave the quiet area around a barcode clear of borders, illustrations and box printing. Print the sample using the actual variable-data process, then scan it after the sticker is applied to the carton. Check the scan result against the visible text and the warehouse record.</p>
  <p>The carton may pass through humid loading areas or rub against other boxes. Test the proposed material and adhesive on the production corrugate after normal taping and stacking. Do not claim universal warehouse durability from a desk sample. If labels are applied manually, the packing team needs a placement reference simple enough to repeat.</p>
  <h2>Control the Last Box in the Run</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send the assembled carton and pallet photos</li><li>Mark tape seams and preferred scan face</li><li>Provide owner-approved SKU and barcode data rules</li><li>Proof longest fields at actual label size</li><li>Scan applied samples from normal working angles</li><li>Define damaged-label reprint and mismatch handling</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-electronics-serial-number-labels-guide/">variable ID guide</a> covers data discipline, and the <a href="/blog/qr-code-stickers-scan-testing-guide/">scan-testing guide</a> covers code verification. Send a carton photo and approved data template through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The carton label's real audience is the person who has to move the right box next.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-swatch-book-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Swatch Book Labels for Samples That Change'
    Description = 'Plan fabric, color or material swatch book labels around sample IDs, handling, removable tabs, small text and version control.'
    Category = 'Sample presentation'
    CardDescription = 'Help a buyer match the physical swatch to the correct SKU after samples are handled and rearranged.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of a fabric and color swatch book with original geometric sample labels showing distinct color names and SKU fields'
    Caption = 'The physical sample needs an identity that stays attached when the collection is passed around.'
    Published = '2026-10-04'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should the sticker go directly on a fabric swatch?', 'Only after testing. Direct contact may affect fabric, finish or removal; a backing card or tab can be safer for some sample systems.'),
      @('Can one label design cover an entire changing collection?', 'A consistent template can, but each variant needs controlled name and SKU data. Reprint or replace outdated identifiers when the collection changes.'),
      @('How small can a swatch label be?', 'Size should be set by the longest real code, readable type and handling method. Print a final-size sample before bulk production.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A swatch book gets opened, passed across a table, closed and reopened weeks later. The buyer may remember a blue fabric but not its code. If the label detaches or the tiny SKU cannot be read, the sample stops helping the sale. I would judge the sticker after several people have handled the book, not only in its new-box photograph.</p>
  <p>Start by identifying where the label belongs: on a backing card, a plastic tab, a sample envelope or the swatch itself. Directly sticking to fabric or a coated finish can alter the material or make removal difficult. The collection owner should decide whether the sample will be reordered, replaced or discarded after use.</p>
  <h2>Give Each Physical Sample a Stable Identity</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Approval question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Swatch name</td><td>Human recognition</td><td>Exact approved spelling</td></tr><tr><td>SKU or material code</td><td>Quotation and reorder</td><td>Unique current identifier</td></tr><tr><td>Collection version</td><td>Separates old from new</td><td>Who updates the book?</td></tr><tr><td>Original color cue</td><td>Visual navigation</td><td>Does it compete with the sample?</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>An original geometric icon can distinguish a family of materials, but the real swatch must remain visible. A decorative sticker should not cover the exact texture or color the buyer is evaluating. Use a consistent placement and leave enough room for the longest plausible code, not just the shortest sample in the first batch.</p>
  <h2>Test Handling and Replacement</h2>
  <p>Make a short prototype book. Turn pages repeatedly, carry it in a bag and let several people pull sample tabs as they normally would. Check whether label edges catch, type rubs away or a code disappears behind a binder ring. If swatches are frequently replaced, trial removal from the chosen backing card or tab without tearing it.</p>
  <p>Printed color on a label is not a guaranteed match to the physical textile, paint or material. Avoid presenting the small sticker as a color standard unless the collection owner has a controlled approval method. The sample itself is the reference; the label supports identity and navigation.</p>
  <h2>Keep the Book Current</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send the actual swatch holder or backing card</li><li>Supply approved names, codes and version rules</li><li>Proof the longest code at final size</li><li>Test repeated page turns and sample removal</li><li>Check labels near binder rings and cutouts</li><li>Define how retired samples and labels are replaced</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> helps control changing SKU files; the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">adhesive guide</a> frames replacement tests. Send a photo of the open book and a sample data sheet through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A useful swatch label protects the connection between what the buyer touched and what the supplier will quote.</p>
'@
  }
)
